    "gold_solution": "```python\nimport math\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"Power_Transmission_Network\")\n    m.setParam(\"TimeLimit\", 300)\n\n    # Problem Data\n    n_nodes = 5\n    nodes = list(range(n_nodes))\n    n_generators = 2\n\n    # Node coordinates (x, y) in km\n    node_coords = [(67, 54), (45, 95), (11, 91), (39, 9), (81, 51)]\n\n    # Node types\n    node_types = ['generator', 'generator', 'consumer', 'consumer', 'consumer']\n\n    # Power demands and generator capacities (MW)\n    power_demands = [0.0, 0.0, 26.0, 26.0, 13.0]\n    generator_capacities = [61.0, 187.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0]\n\n    # Wire physical properties (INDUCED PARAMETERS)\n    resistivity = 1.68e-08  # Ohm-meter\n    wire_area_mm2 = 500.0  # mm^2\n    wire_area_m2 = wire_area_mm2 * 1e-6  # Convert to m^2\n\n    # Operating parameters\n    operating_voltage = 220.0  # kV\n    line_capacity = 100.0  # MW\n    build_cost_per_km = 100000.0  # $/km\n    loss_cost_rate = 50.0  # $/MWh equivalent\n\n    # COMPUTE distance and resistance matrices (INDUCED COSTS)\n    # Using Ohm's Law: R = resistivity * length / area\n    def compute_distance_km(coord1, coord2):\n        return math.sqrt((coord1[0] - coord2[0])**2 + (coord1[1] - coord2[1])**2)\n\n    def compute_resistance(dist_km):\n        dist_m = dist_km * 1000  # Convert km to m\n        return resistivity * dist_m / wire_area_m2\n\n    distances = {}  # km\n    resistances = {}  # Ohm\n    for i in nodes:\n        for j in nodes:\n            if i != j:\n                dist = compute_distance_km(node_coords[i], node_coords[j])\n                distances[(i, j)] = dist\n                resistances[(i, j)] = compute_resistance(dist)\n\n    # Create edge list (undirected, i < j)\n    edges = [(i, j) for i in nodes for j in nodes if i < j]\n\n    # Decision Variables\n    # Build_i_j: Binary - whether to build line between nodes i and j\n    build = {}\n    for (i, j) in edges:\n        build[(i, j)] = m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f\"Build_{i}_{j}\")\n\n    # Flow_i_j: Continuous - power flow from node i to j (MW)\n    flow = {}\n    for i in nodes:\n        for j in nodes:\n            if i != j:\n                flow[(i, j)] = m.addVar(lb=0.0, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Flow_{i}_{j}\")\n\n    # Helper function to get build variable (handles i > j case)\n    def get_build(i, j):\n        if i < j:\n            return build[(i, j)]\n        else:\n            return build[(j, i)]\n\n    # Objective: Minimize construction cost + operational transmission cost\n    # Cost factor: converts resistance to cost coefficient\n    # Cost = LossRate * (R / V^2) * P^2\n    # factor = LossRate / V^2\n    loss_factor = loss_cost_rate / (operating_voltage ** 2)\n\n    # Construction costs (based on distance)\n    obj = quicksum(distances[(i, j)] * build_cost_per_km * build[(i, j)] for (i, j) in edges)\n\n    # Operational costs: Quadratic (I^2*R losses)\n    # Note: Gurobi supports quadratic objectives directly\n    obj += quicksum(\n        resistances[(i, j)] * loss_factor * flow[(i, j)] * flow[(i, j)]\n        for i in nodes for j in nodes if i != j\n    )\n    m.setObjective(obj, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # Flow conservation constraints\n    for node in nodes:\n        inflow = quicksum(flow[(other, node)] for other in nodes if other != node)\n        outflow = quicksum(flow[(node, other)] for other in nodes if other != node)\n        \n        if node_types[node] == 'generator':\n            # Generator: net outflow (generation) <= capacity\n            # Rewritten: inflow - outflow >= -capacity\n            m.addConstr(\n                inflow - outflow >= -generator_capacities[node],\n                name=f\"Gen_Cap_{node}\"\n            )\n        else:\n            # Consumer: net inflow >= demand\n            m.addConstr(\n                inflow - outflow >= power_demands[node],\n                name=f\"Demand_{node}\"\n            )\n\n    # Capacity constraints: Flow <= line_capacity * Build\n    for i in nodes:\n        for j in nodes:\n            if i != j:\n                m.addConstr(\n                    flow[(i, j)] <= line_capacity * get_build(i, j),\n                    name=f\"Cap_{i}_{j}\"\n                )\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "Subject: Planning memo \u2014 design of a five\u2011node transmission network to connect generators to consumers\n\nWe have been asked to plan a small transmission network that links two generator sites to three consumer locations. The five geographic locations are defined by map coordinates in Node Coordinates (km):\n  - N0 (generator): (67, 54)\n  - N1 (generator): (45, 95)\n  - N2 (consumer): (11, 91)\n  - N3 (consumer): (39, 9)\n  - N4 (consumer): (81, 51), and the role of each location (generator or consumer) is identified in ['generator', 'generator', 'consumer', 'consumer', 'consumer']. Consumer power requirements are provided in Consumer demands: N2: 26.0 MW, N3: 26.0 MW, N4: 13.0 MW, and the maximum available output at the generator sites is given in Generator capacities: N0: 61.0 MW, N1: 187.0 MW.\n\nDesign flexibility and physical scope\n- There are no predefined corridors or rights-of-way for this study. We may choose to build a transmission span between any pair of nodes, using straight\u2011line distance on the map as the basis for length.\n- Any line we decide to build can carry power in either direction; however, it must respect a thermal transfer rating of 100.0 MW in each direction.\n- The wire material is copper. The material property for the conductor is specified in 1.68e-08 \u03a9\u00b7m (copper), and the intended conductor size is given by 500.0 mm\u00b2. These choices determine the per\u2011kilometer resistance of any new span, which scales with length and inversely with cross\u2011section.\n\nCost model and why it matters\n- Capital cost: Building a new span incurs a one\u2011time cost proportional to length, at the rate in $100,000/km. Because we can connect any pair of nodes, shorter spans generally cost less, but they may not be the most economical when operational losses are considered.\n- Operational \u201cheating\u201d cost: When we move power through a copper line, resistive losses occur and show up as heat. These losses follow a square law with respect to power flow. In practical terms, if we double the power on a span, the resistive losses\u2014and therefore the ongoing cost tied to those losses\u2014go up by about four times. We value this energy loss using $50.0/MWh.\n- The square law is driven by physics: loss is proportional to line resistance and to the square of current. At a given power transfer, the current depends on the operating level we choose. With the system operated at 220.0 kV, the loss cost on a span scales with Resistance \u00d7 (Power/Voltage)\u00b2. This means higher voltage strongly suppresses heating costs via a 1/V\u00b2 effect, and heavier (lower\u2011resistance) conductors or shorter routes also help.\n\nPower delivery requirements and planning assumptions\n- Each consumer must receive enough power to meet its stated demand. Generator outputs must not exceed their stated maxima.\n- Power is conserved at each node in the planning sense: the total sent into a node equals the total taken out when you look only at transfers between nodes. We treat resistive losses as a separate financial penalty rather than deducting them from nodal balances for this study\u2019s bookkeeping. In other words, the network must route enough power to cover the consumers\u2019 needs, and we pay for the associated heating losses on top.\n\nWhat we must decide\n- Which transmission spans to build between the five nodes.\n- How to route power flows across the built spans, within the per\u2011span rating of 100.0 MW, so that each consumer\u2019s demand is met without exceeding generator limits and while respecting the bidirectional nature of each span.\n- The solution should minimize the combined total of construction spending and the ongoing cost of resistive losses. Because losses rise with the square of flow, this is not a problem that can be handled by simply summing flows; we must account for the nonlinear escalation in heating costs. The copper conductor properties in 1.68e-08 \u03a9\u00b7m (copper) and 500.0 mm\u00b2, the selected transmission level 220.0 kV, the span lengths derived from Node Coordinates (km):\n  - N0 (generator): (67, 54)\n  - N1 (generator): (45, 95)\n  - N2 (consumer): (11, 91)\n  - N3 (consumer): (39, 9)\n  - N4 (consumer): (81, 51), and the valuation in $50.0/MWh will jointly determine the operational cost component.\n\nIn short, our task is to design a cost\u2011effective set of lines and power routings that connects two generators to three consumers, meeting all power needs while keeping both construction spending and square\u2011law heating costs as low as possible. The planning treatment of losses ensures we capture their real economic impact without complicating the nodal accounting, and the chosen voltage level and conductor sizing will be key levers in mitigating those quadratic losses.",
    "LLM_description_template": "Subject: Planning memo \u2014 design of a five\u2011node transmission network to connect generators to consumers\n\nWe have been asked to plan a small transmission network that links two generator sites to three consumer locations. The five geographic locations are defined by map coordinates in {NODE_COORDINATES}, and the role of each location (generator or consumer) is identified in {NODE_TYPES}. Consumer power requirements are provided in {POWER_DEMANDS}, and the maximum available output at the generator sites is given in {GENERATOR_CAPACITIES}.\n\nDesign flexibility and physical scope\n- There are no predefined corridors or rights-of-way for this study. We may choose to build a transmission span between any pair of nodes, using straight\u2011line distance on the map as the basis for length.\n- Any line we decide to build can carry power in either direction; however, it must respect a thermal transfer rating of {LINE_CAPACITY} in each direction.\n- The wire material is copper. The material property for the conductor is specified in {WIRE_RESISTIVITY}, and the intended conductor size is given by {WIRE_CROSS_SECTION}. These choices determine the per\u2011kilometer resistance of any new span, which scales with length and inversely with cross\u2011section.\n\nCost model and why it matters\n- Capital cost: Building a new span incurs a one\u2011time cost proportional to length, at the rate in {BUILD_COST_PER_KM}. Because we can connect any pair of nodes, shorter spans generally cost less, but they may not be the most economical when operational losses are considered.\n- Operational \u201cheating\u201d cost: When we move power through a copper line, resistive losses occur and show up as heat. These losses follow a square law with respect to power flow. In practical terms, if we double the power on a span, the resistive losses\u2014and therefore the ongoing cost tied to those losses\u2014go up by about four times. We value this energy loss using {LOSS_COST_RATE}.\n- The square law is driven by physics: loss is proportional to line resistance and to the square of current. At a given power transfer, the current depends on the operating level we choose. With the system operated at {OPERATING_VOLTAGE}, the loss cost on a span scales with Resistance \u00d7 (Power/Voltage)\u00b2. This means higher voltage strongly suppresses heating costs via a 1/V\u00b2 effect, and heavier (lower\u2011resistance) conductors or shorter routes also help.\n\nPower delivery requirements and planning assumptions\n- Each consumer must receive enough power to meet its stated demand. Generator outputs must not exceed their stated maxima.\n- Power is conserved at each node in the planning sense: the total sent into a node equals the total taken out when you look only at transfers between nodes. We treat resistive losses as a separate financial penalty rather than deducting them from nodal balances for this study\u2019s bookkeeping. In other words, the network must route enough power to cover the consumers\u2019 needs, and we pay for the associated heating losses on top.\n\nWhat we must decide\n- Which transmission spans to build between the five nodes.\n- How to route power flows across the built spans, within the per\u2011span rating of {LINE_CAPACITY}, so that each consumer\u2019s demand is met without exceeding generator limits and while respecting the bidirectional nature of each span.\n- The solution should minimize the combined total of construction spending and the ongoing cost of resistive losses. Because losses rise with the square of flow, this is not a problem that can be handled by simply summing flows; we must account for the nonlinear escalation in heating costs. The copper conductor properties in {WIRE_RESISTIVITY} and {WIRE_CROSS_SECTION}, the selected transmission level {OPERATING_VOLTAGE}, the span lengths derived from {NODE_COORDINATES}, and the valuation in {LOSS_COST_RATE} will jointly determine the operational cost component.\n\nIn short, our task is to design a cost\u2011effective set of lines and power routings that connects two generators to three consumers, meeting all power needs while keeping both construction spending and square\u2011law heating costs as low as possible. The planning treatment of losses ensures we capture their real economic impact without complicating the nodal accounting, and the chosen voltage level and conductor sizing will be key levers in mitigating those quadratic losses."
}